  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nWe propose a multi-scale optimization strategy that adapts the grid resolution based on solution quality, escaping local optima. The core idea is to:\n- Confine local optimization to regions where the functional value is close to the parent's value\n- Use the parent solution as an anchor for both constraints and initialization\n- Monitor solution quality during optimization to decide grid progression\n- Progressively increase resolution only if improvements are found\n\nThis prevents: 1) Premature grid doubling without improvement, 2) Wasted computations on grids yielding worse solutions, 3) Getting trapped in local optima near the parent. Limitations: Grid-level runtime complexity still dominates, and we cannot guarantee finding the global optimum.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nVariables:\n- h \u2208 \u211d\u207f: step function values at \u2080\u2264x\u2264\u2082 samples with n_points = 2N samples\n- dx = 2.0/(2N) = 1.0/N: grid spacing (lattice formation)\n- C_5 = (MAX_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx) = (CORR \u22c5 dx): overlap constant\n\nObjective:\nMinimize C_5(h) = max_{k=-(N-1)}^{N-1} |\u222ch(x)(1-h(x+k))dx|\n\nConstraints:\n\u2200i \u2208 1..n_points: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n\u222bh(x)dx = 1 \u21d2 \u2211h = N (with lattice spacing 1/N)\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\nStage 1: Grid Setup\n- Initialize with parent solution (n=47 -> N=24)\n- Project parent to target grid N_grid = 24 * scale_factor\n- Set initial best solution = parent\n\nStage 2: Descent Method\nUse gradient-based optimization with:\n- Objective: C_5(h) via direct correlation computation\n- Constraints: Sum(h) = target_sum (projected via Lagrange multipliers)\n- Bounds: Box constraints (0,1)\n\nModified Gradient Step:\nfor i in range(max_iter):\n  \u03c6 = N_grid*(average h)\n  grad = project_gradient(project_objective(h, N_grid), \u03c6)\n  h = max(0, min(1, h - learning_rate * grad))\n\nStage 3: Descent Condition Check\nMonitor decrease in C_5 and normalized distance to parent:\nif \u0394C_5 < 1e-4 * min_C and ||h-parent|| > 1e-3*N_grid:\n  break and double grid\n\nStage 4: Grid Projection\nFrom h_old (n_old points) to h_new (n_new points):\nx_old = generalized_interpolation(h_old, N_old)\nh_new = re_project(x_old, N_new)\n\nStage 5: Dual Verification\nRun two local optimizations per grid:\n1. Local improvement around parent\n2. Strategic perturbation (e.g., block inversion)\n\nReturn best solution across all grids.\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n- Grid progression: Fractional scaling (1.5-1.8x) not pure doubling\n- Optimization settings:\n  - Initial learning_rate = 0.01 (adaptive increase)\n  - Max_iter=100 ($O(1e3)$ function evaluations)\n  - Sum constraint resolution: Use projection method to maintain feasibility\n- Restart strategy:\n  - When stuck, perform random walk with step = 0.2*(1/N_grid)\n  - Maximum restarts=3 per grid iteration\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n- Primary grid (N=24): expected ~100s\n- Secondary grid (N=36): ~300s\n- Time-strict mode: \n  - Set iteration limit instead of time limit\n  - Use dimensionality-reduced checks for unnecessary computations\n- Deadline handling:\n  - Store periodic checkpoints after each grid's best solution\n  - Allow grid reduction back to 24 to resume interrupted searches\n  - Early termination returns best solution found\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n- 0 <= h[i] <= 1: Enforce via clamping and scaling\n- Exact sum constraint: $(\u2211h = N_grids)$ (integral invariant)\n- Objective consistency: Recompute with same correlation method before returning\n- Final candidate must be:\n  1. Feasible w.r. Box constraints\n  2. Meet integral requirement (|sum(h) - N| < 1e-5)\n  3. Return (h, C_5_val, n_points) where n_points = 2*N_grid\n  4. Handle abrupt termination by restoring best known solution",
